#{[{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162857} :activity/id #uuid "5f2d7b62-9c95-5591-9da4-e8e5d9022441" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162857 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163232 :tutorial/content "The **singular value decomposition** (or SVD) of an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ is a factorization of the form\n\n$$\nA=U \\Sigma V^T,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\Sigma$ is an $m \\times n$ matrix with the singular values of $A$ (ordered from largest to smallest) on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $m \\times m$ matrix, and\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $n \\times n$ matrix.\n\nThe columns of $U$ are called the **left singular vectors** of $A,$ and the columns of $V$ are called the **right singular vectors** of $A.$\n\nFor example, the matrix ![](images/46/46b29ceb84e2ed98e48642e67277c9bf63d0552bcd07f813a9ffcaf665ff91b7.svg) has the following singular value decomposition:\n\n![](images/29/298933eb0a789ebe57a296088172f6927d1573770824c0b0cffb393eb9894c70.svg)\n\nThe singular value decomposition of a matrix $A$ can be thought of as a type of diagonalization that works for *any* $m\\times n$ matrix! It's also one of linear algebra's most useful forms of matrix factorization with applications in statistics, image compression, machine learning, and more." :tutorial/id #uuid "ae8b1a4c-c37e-5527-9f72-9cf20f5af926" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4892 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "db915660-02e7-5288-8506-ad3f2aa2ddf5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162908}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162908 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162016 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c94ec9c4-650f-5d39-9294-651b03b172bf"} :step/id #uuid "486b32a1-b52c-5baa-bdcc-412851ba4a9e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162913}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162909 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162017 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0d7f5df-a1f9-5242-9386-446313ec0178"} :step/id #uuid "3b004c12-845e-56ca-9f93-dabde8cab0c8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162912}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162910 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162018 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02dfe937-e604-5985-af6f-cdfa1a574ccd"} :step/id #uuid "3da79aa6-a10d-52b5-a714-6b64af170db3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162914}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162911 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162019 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9466d5b-d361-5504-8e16-99f87210e808"} :step/id #uuid "5eeddc56-d36b-512a-a9d9-5d2227617238" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163155}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162912 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163246 :tutorial/content "We can now fully describe the algorithm for computing the singular value decomposition of $A.$\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A.$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values. This allows us to construct the second matrix $\\Sigma.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$). This allows us to construct the third matrix $V.$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$ using the singular values, right singular vectors, and the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_i = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma_i} A\\mathbf{v}_i,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $\\mathbf u_i$ and $\\mathbf v_i$ are the $i$th columns of the matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively. This allows us to construct the matrix $U.$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $\\Sigma$ shown below. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ let's find the matrices $U$ and $V.$\n\n![](images/ab/abe87fb2a304ff102bb3f703d025c7d41f56cb6fab7fc477b3073cbd820cda6e.svg)\n\nWe find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using our algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & =[\\begin{aligned}89 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 113\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that $\\color{blue}\\sigma_1=11$ and $\\color{red}\\sigma_2=9.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$):\n\n- Seeking a unit solution of $(A^T \\! A - 121I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives\n![](images/41/4195807c3f61cd730c57340666c8d651e94d2b277f9623da7cd482c1d7123303.svg)\n\n- Seeking a unit solution of $(A^T \\! A - 81I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives\n![](images/64/64aa31f039ec188355522cffa8a6c62df8c53bb409e55df32ce880d9a9efcd6e.svg)\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/ab/ab0a5a5a284c962414158e5b7c463264b271bdd57decd8e08305285f7243f7bd.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "4b48ac70-7c4e-59be-bdfb-64125ae50eec" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5010 :tutorial/title "An Algorithm for Finding a Singular Value Decomposition"} :step/id #uuid "c0a6dc33-d7cd-5300-a862-451162ab2de3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162911}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162913 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163247 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix, and \n\n$$\nA= U\\Sigma V^T\n$$\n\nis a singular value decomposition of $A.$ Let's describe how to find the matrices $V$ and $\\Sigma$ of this singular value decomposition. \n\nNotice that if we compute $A^TA,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={(UΣ{V}^{T})}^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =(V{Σ}^{T}\\,{U}^{T})(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}\\,U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}({I}_{n})Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V({Σ}^{T}\\,Σ){V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we note that $A^T \\! A$ is always symmetric, and that $\\Sigma^T \\! \\Sigma$ is a diagonal matrix with the squares of the singular values on the main diagonal. As a result,\n\n$$\nA^TA = V\\left( \\Sigma^T \\Sigma\\right) V^T\n$$\n\nis an orthogonal diagonalization of the symmetric matrix $A^T \\! A.$ So, to find the matrix $V$ in our SVD, we need to orthogonally diagonalize $A^T A.$\n\nLet's demonstrate how this works in practice by computing the matrices $\\Sigma$ and $V$ in the SVD of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/b7/b766ec7d8c00dfc121e707566d21db470c087c3e785f4e1b2868e7f932873c67.svg)\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- Computing $A^T \\! A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 13\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Solving the characteristic equation $|A^T \\! A - \\lambda I| = 0,$ we get the eigenvalues of $A^T \\! A \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{\\lambda_1 = 16}}, \\qquad  {\\color{red}{\\lambda_2 = 1}}\n$$\n\nSo, the corresponding singular values are \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\sigma_1 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_1}  = 4},  \\qquad {\\color{red}\\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_2} = 1}.\n$$\n\nWe now find a unit eigenvector basis of $A^T \\! A.$ These unit eigenvectors will be the columns of our matrix $V$ in the orthogonal diagonalization of $A^T \\! A$.\n\n- Computing $(A^T \\! A - {\\color{blue}\\lambda_1} I),$ we obtain\n![](images/f0/f074943374fd488663eda967ab64f335047f9b6cc91c98c504dcb0f2ee8282a6.svg)\nSeeking a unit solution of $(A^T\\! A - 16I)\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0},$ we get\n![](images/03/03d6a116d751736f50c366cd4721ee7d1dbf39048092272acc6577934f4600c7.svg)\n\n- Computing $(A^T \\! A - {\\color{red}\\lambda_2} I),$ we obtain\n![](images/16/16813fa19b3417b6de49f9c3c1c27cfedca850b473da01d3b0e0a0b3091b6e28.svg)\nSeeking a unit solution of $(A^T\\! A - I)\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0},$ we get\n![](images/32/32b3c9740740767d4d849ac2d73552dee1e225cc507e95a17c727e8c3fcc6275.svg)\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $\\Sigma$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/85/85c926521f0588117992c019ab0db3723520a14ba4c2879d630ab391462b4af2.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "bbaa68b2-45d9-56a5-99b7-90dce5ec08da" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5011 :tutorial/title "Finding the Second and Third Matrices of a Singular Value Decomposition"} :step/id #uuid "c7ff20bf-405e-5d22-a1a1-24febcd398fc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162910}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162914 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163248 :tutorial/content "Suppose that ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is an ![](images/25/2521651122403a440e682fef383af333958f5b0d1334abe8b0771865d4daf100.svg) matrix, and \n\n![](images/ec/ec033e8518a97ff363494df7effa3c6f032b7cfaa028712b87959d659ea12962.svg)\nis a singular value decomposition of ![](images/c8/c8c72090d28b539bed2177052b06540383d63ac720008f1b301dafcac4fa6227.svg) Let's now describe how to compute the matrix ![](images/c3/c3ae10e66e0dee45c359329a1cd8b0246634f95fdce2f5bf206d04dac3594fc5.svg) of the SVD using the matrices ![](images/12/128e4b474ccbb86ef9b1a105a348e493595f4084d05e9cfa5f7889d390d5eac4.svg) and ![](images/4d/4d936a1af541b15fe85bff8b60fbe1d8d1851f9e91616cf4799dd0c5323d038a.svg)\n\nRecall that ![](images/be/bef27a67f1f86637c8224c0d929308a97b23aad62dfbbc5549c68e12bb94f143.svg) is orthogonal, meaning that ![](images/c7/c71eaab408a38ca1a1d0c653fe51ab02287d8ffbe4ef0461c93186579552d456.svg) Consequently, multiplying ![](images/c9/c99b0facd0ff831056deed9aaa5356ca5cbeed72ed9f632604cd5fb9479c3b7a.svg) by ![](images/be/bef27a67f1f86637c8224c0d929308a97b23aad62dfbbc5549c68e12bb94f143.svg) on the right, we obtain\n\n![](images/03/036ae766d4187370271793b6bbacc9841b444173ff031740acbd259531ed5126.svg)\n\nIn expanded form, our final equation looks as follows:\n\n![](images/b9/b924929217b058d3edefff9a6557d3ad8b5d6b3536e7ac0dcaf185e50b60bb5a.svg)\n\nTherefore,\n\n![](images/64/64b1897c8532aee8fee0d4f76f632cc91c6a5a4078b11bfaaea07235aeadcc3f.svg)\n\nfor ![](images/fe/fedbe16eb8c09802bf435c256bb225249c1ee578dd558d0f3b6b284a56236517.svg) Finally, provided that ![](images/66/668ce05b1a81d3d120c47a7dadea446891ef986edb5f499c592f310755bb143c.svg) we obtain \n\n![](images/ec/ecd9fe71eaef2172512d70bfb78ffea6e36b973e6f96c09f0480ef2e0e9e55ad.svg) \n\nWe can use this to find the columns of the matrix ![](images/f9/f9d412fae57d02f4fc44b04a277e1a6512e3f560e3152416a69d30d72e38a9ac.svg) Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "b577630f-1060-52c1-aabe-91b641046fa5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5012 :tutorial/title "Finding the First Matrix of a Singular Value Decomposition"} :step/id #uuid "e1d953a4-69ca-563a-afef-090b7f058ef3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163053}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163053 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163317 :tutorial/content "Consider the matrices $A,$ $\\Sigma,$ and $V$ shown below. \n\n![](images/61/61ff6d3a83c2a660ca8369d96ab7bede86f9a124727db8d5a7e320dcd5aaad74.svg)\n\nSuppose we're given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A.$ Let's use this information to find the matrix $U.$\n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that the singular values are $\\color{blue}\\sigma_1=9$ and $\\color{red}\\sigma_2=1.$ \n\nEarlier, we saw that the columns of $\\mathbf u$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_i = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma_i} A\\mathbf{v}_i\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u_i$ and $\\mathbf v_i$ are the $i$th columns of the matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively. Therefore, we compute the columns of $\\mathbf u$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute the vector $\\mathbf u_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{27}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{9}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the vector $\\mathbf u_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the matrix $U$ in our singular value decomposition of $A$ is given by\n\n![](images/15/159ace613dacf666b32131543c77b3eb602e16226ba2d37e9e8bea4dcff1cb56.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "04644a93-8cce-5c2e-b575-d7f05b4985df" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6135 :tutorial/title "Calculating the First Matrix - A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "9c32bd79-d939-5ba0-afca-75b88e74b8b9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162909}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163155 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163364 :tutorial/content "Let $A = U \\Sigma V^T$ be a singular value decomposition of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ with singular values $\\sigma_1$ and $\\sigma_2.$ \n\nDue to the decomposition, the action of $A$ on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ is the same as the action of the sequence $V^T \\to \\Sigma \\to U.$\n\nAs shown below, let's consider both equivalent actions on a unit circle (with the two standard basis vectors). \n\n![](images/18/18bf28d59138e6c50e950493570a4e9caa8ec2f86eb81d573272cb4677ea1d69.png)\n\n- Since $V^T$ is an orthogonal $2 \\times 2$ matrix, it represents a transformation of the plane that preserves distances between vectors. This could be a rotation, a reflection, or a combination of these two, which is sometimes called a **rotoreflection.**\n\n- Since $\\Sigma$ is a diagonal matrix, it represents a scaling along the $x$- and $y$-axes by $\\sigma_1$ and $\\sigma_2,$ respectively.\n\n- Since $U$ is again an orthogonal $2 \\times 2$ matrix, it represents a rotation, a reflection, or a rotoreflection.\n\nTo summarize, we have the following theorem:\n\n*Any linear transformation on the plane can be decomposed into an orthogonal transformation (rotation, reflection, or rotoreflection), a scaling, and another orthogonal transformation (rotation, reflection, or rotoreflection).*" :tutorial/id #uuid "4cadc01a-8396-532a-a438-479affeb9a04" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7033 :tutorial/title "Geometric Interpretation of the Singular Value Decomposition"} :step/id #uuid "7cc03796-6cdf-582b-9c6a-edc187a827b2"}] :activity/title "Singular Value Decomposition of 2x2 Matrices" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162226} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428020} :activity/id #uuid "be1bd01d-d4e3-5a46-9543-53b198bb6268" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428016 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371360 :question/id #uuid "9e6bed9f-9e8e-54a8-ba26-f0e8954ce7e0"} :step/id #uuid "0c463439-a38b-593e-b012-0bd4d757c8b7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428019}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428017 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404836 :question/id #uuid "069d96cb-6a82-58f7-9653-ba67b55165b9"} :step/id #uuid "42130988-8a9c-5c98-b57a-c5c217558433" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428018}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428018 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428015 :question/id #uuid "39a1a8bd-f69f-5f36-99bc-5bd95f9b782d"} :step/id #uuid "b772e15d-f5b9-5724-9449-b771545c21bc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428016}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428019 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404891 :question/id #uuid "5095a12c-e6f9-56a4-8ce5-1c1e4698b3dc"} :step/id #uuid "bc5679a7-ffcd-561e-8c7e-fd7ddec627e4"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428020 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371283 :question/id #uuid "7f6a8969-0c0e-547e-babb-6ff2c612e774"} :step/id #uuid "eb2350bf-19b6-5869-b8de-89f0f6ea18f9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428017}}] :activity/title "Singular Valu