\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $V?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that since $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ the matrix $V$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$ has both properties."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496292 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "285a9780-e86c-548a-adb5-cbb884e8ca6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495598} {:answer/id #uuid "2d03c4f6-f195-5e6b-866b-9bb346f9fc68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495611} {:answer/id #uuid "319bb27d-e8dd-5212-8869-65e8ae228b7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495624} {:answer/id #uuid "ea634426-335f-5311-b804-0a4c4997b3bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496027} {:answer/id #uuid "f0ec482c-c5db-502f-b6f8-1aae7843448d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495624} :answer-field/id #uuid "d36a171f-653b-5a9e-b0c3-ea8ba71c938e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496186}] :question/id #uuid "a0773915-2806-5187-a512-1daa0afdcd4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-96118" :question/problem "Given the singular value decomposition $A=UΣ{V}^{T},$ where $A$ is a $3\\times 5$ matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $U$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix\n\nII. $U$ is an orthogonal matrix\n\nIII. $Σ$ is an orthogonal matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $m\\times n$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $m\\times m$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $n\\times n$ matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $A$ is a $3\\times 5$ matrix, $U$ must be a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the matrix $U$ in the singular value decomposition $UΣ{V}^{T}$ must be orthogonal.\n\n- Statement III is false. The matrix $Σ$ must be diagonal but not necessarily orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496294 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4abf63e6-f726-5e62-877b-e55ec936e71c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495683} {:answer/id #uuid "94c97f0b-950e-53ee-8528-58cf113935e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495839} {:answer/id #uuid "9aea7879-dd37-548f-a388-79a0c1cba990" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495855} {:answer/id #uuid "cecc1d54-52d9-5a5f-8a1d-bfaee1aac033" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495960} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c11591-dfe6-5782-9a57-f4732952a8b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495683} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8e0de73-fab0-5dbe-bb6a-4caec9eea734" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496192}] :question/id #uuid "a6cc8412-6a80-579d-ba3c-43e9e90968b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-96232" :question/problem "$$\nV=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,∗\\, & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\,∗\\, & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $V?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that since $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ the matrix $V$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$ has both properties."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543517 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4590f3bd-e752-5e65-a452-9b1d195950f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542828} {:answer/id #uuid "4ee1bbdc-7319-50f5-b95b-e0b9229082a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542855} {:answer/id #uuid "5d4fe57c-b619-5bf4-a435-2a7ba27bfb30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542889} {:answer/id #uuid "75c1a5b8-85c2-5eb5-b5b9-c7bab8ab41a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542951} {:answer/id #uuid "87a762ea-1f1b-52db-9e42-9df82522b93d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542998} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d2a03a5-a99e-5c9a-aa6c-8403dc252051" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543399}] :question/id #uuid "75ae4937-7257-5ddb-963c-2c5dab077964" :question/math-academy-id "q-140635" :question/problem "Given the singular value decomposition $A=UΣ{V}^{T},$ where $A$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $U$ is a $4\\times 4$ orthogonal matrix\n\nII. $Σ$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\nIII. $V$ is a diagonal matrix with only positive elements on the main diagonal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $m\\times n$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $m\\times m$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $n\\times n$ matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix, $U$ in the singular value decomposition $UΣ{V}^{T}$ must be a $4\\times 4$ orthogonal matrix.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $A$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix, $Σ$ must be a $4\\times 2$ matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. The matrix $V$ in the singular value decomposition $UΣ{V}^{T}$ must be an orthogonal matrix, not a diagonal matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Singular Value Decomposition"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162017 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161809 :question/id #uuid "ad5570e6-4810-5b07-a676-711f0fd069a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-8414" :question/problem "![](images/f0/f0cb7d70ea0921d1e2e8f2f0a9d8bc06427f29e28b8ed6ea79b5db4ba132f1e0.svg)\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $\\Sigma,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and $\\sigma_i \\neq 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_i = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma_i} A\\mathbf{v}_i,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $\\mathbf v_i$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that the singular values are $\\sigma_1=9$ and $\\sigma_2=4.$ Therefore, the second column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{12}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0d7f5df-a1f9-5242-9386-446313ec0178" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162666 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54deaa5d-b42c-582b-add4-d6575e45a079" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520120} {:answer/id #uuid "5adca1a5-04fa-5979-ab07-c4374ef39ef9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520132} {:answer/id #uuid "8584a18f-938b-5b11-9a92-aea06357491c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520185} {:answer/id #uuid "875c7469-79dd-5306-8869-1ae4d067e4bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520190} {:answer/id #uuid "e5b0b4f8-5d8c-5df5-bcb5-6a41d1765e47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520326}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520120} :answer-field/id #uuid "50d8446e-fd5b-543b-bc96-f048b71511a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161288}] :question/id #uuid "1290450b-3f65-5ec4-8f81-47e7a7dc9fcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-95734" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=6$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$ Therefore, the second column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162682 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ba44f9d-452d-5c1b-a60d-1fa5aefc80dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418098} {:answer/id #uuid "780c2e8d-9e3d-5f3e-8162-9411863f1b43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418112} {:answer/id #uuid "82cad0d2-c8f3-56cd-8aac-7b3ceabb2dc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418118} {:answer/id #uuid "987dbc1f-e284-5329-a013-0a5967179f36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418122} {:answer/id #uuid "eccfbb1d-63d6-5847-8131-30535d50b607" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418137} :answer-field/id #uuid "c09611e5-05b8-5cb4-967b-28cb8b87db79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418148}] :question/id #uuid "f9d078bc-6d79-5354-a3a2-388a6b495358" :question/math-academy-id "q-96317" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 \\\\ 7 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=8.$ Therefore, the second column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 \\\\ 7 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ -\\frac{32}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369804 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dea2437-4ccf-502f-90d7-8d9c2ee56bf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369806} {:answer/id #uuid "6085e643-d1c0-5b27-a31d-c8f17d3991e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369807} {:answer/id #uuid "fd3669b4-5040-571b-913c-4c7797a98323" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369808} {:answer/id #uuid "71d09b33-1adc-5eee-9aef-23379e505ecf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt