\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{27}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418158 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b06010d-965b-582a-8b2a-49ead930bd31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418092} {:answer/id #uuid "0f85dcb3-9485-53ae-ae8a-22f225ec6ba6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418093} {:answer/id #uuid "3eb57cdc-06ed-5be6-a085-358bde4fb1c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418099} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb86930-f90d-5b12-90e9-e48a9d51a232" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418106} {:answer/id #uuid "f788dd4c-e41a-55fe-ab74-c1d4c2ae177a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418099} :answer-field/id #uuid "57b6d338-830b-5ed8-8f0a-f64dd8753dcc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418143}] :question/id #uuid "b33c0902-677a-58a2-8ff6-887b643a37e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-96309" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=7$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=6.$ Therefore, the second column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ae24d11-1cd8-53d0-ad47-5b285f4d0b75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495605} {:answer/id #uuid "67a53e3a-1703-5b02-8afb-8f54ac862fec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495739} {:answer/id #uuid "68598054-f8a2-58e6-b3cf-31bcc49b1676" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495742} {:answer/id #uuid "97e9def0-f134-5fa8-8ec0-c84afe6f5876" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495850} {:answer/id #uuid "fadf9222-3d73-5771-87bf-4aacd4a26f7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495742} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3d56696-4ed6-5583-85eb-387af7ed31be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496201}] :question/id #uuid "79aa6457-0ad1-5b78-8ab5-2196791a63cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-95906" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$ Therefore, the second column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Columns of the First Matrix Given Part of a Singular Value Decomposition"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162018 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161810 :question/id #uuid "d861ebc5-8e1e-5cf0-9b82-5a64ff80cf96" :question/math-academy-id "e-8415" :question/problem "![](images/cb/cb8af25c129aac1ef9268d108c073e4b895f5e154c26ddb5692fd527d4eaddc3.svg)\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|v_{11}|+|v_{21}|?$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix $A^T \\! A.$\n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that the singular values are $\\sigma_1=5$ and $\\sigma_2=3.$ So, the first column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value $\\sigma_1=5.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of $A^T \\!A$ corresponding to $\\lambda_1 = \\sigma_1^2=25.$ Computing $A^T \\! A - 25 I,$ we get\n\n![](images/8c/8c5b88c719ee0e5495e9b0df2c9f4add97a668e74249680fbf378c7c775d92f2.svg)\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T\\!A-25I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/b3/b36812322e6f9128de625a8a2d4e7faf62ba98fb007224c819e2a05738cd7f98.svg) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm, we get \n![](images/16/1608541af7edcbbb06504c97b907dc2c40a97d9a7c9575d5145c67785f9eb56c.svg)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|v_{11}|+|v_{21}| = \\left| \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\right| + \\left| \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\right| = \\sqrt{2}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02dfe937-e604-5985-af6f-cdfa1a574ccd" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162663 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c7f71ea-d24e-580b-adf3-8a8a2ff8d4eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161271} {:answer/id #uuid "adf11ea5-82f8-55dc-b479-11a197fd3a97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161272} {:answer/id #uuid "bff79571-2bb9-532e-8e89-90f36adb5fe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{13}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161273} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3ea963-5181-54ac-81d1-b52d30537489" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161274} {:answer/id #uuid "4b9993b7-8a29-55dd-857d-6e263e2eb972" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161271} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d12cea6-72a7-5022-a590-7c09e6cf36dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161270}] :question/id #uuid "66132b20-c86b-5419-aeff-ce901dcebd6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-95717" :question/problem "$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}13 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 13\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{11} & {v}_{12} \\\\ {v}_{21} & {v}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|?$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$ So, the first column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{1}=5.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=25.$ Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-25I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-25I=[\\begin{aligned}13 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 13\\end{aligned}]-25[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-12 & -12 \\\\ -12 & -12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-25I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|=|-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162664 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f091165-8723-50ab-b788-42eed3a5af97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{13}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161277} {:answer/id #uuid "c59a886f-a8e9-5c1a-83c5-0a1690532273" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161278} {:answer/id #uuid "c75b2b50-3ec3-5808-b5ec-a1a4851138b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161279} {:answer/id #uuid "e1b5d11f-d085-5957-ab58-25b88f1e8f53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161280} {:answer/id #uuid "4f8dbebe-20bc-5b8e-a384-b6ffc2c89a0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161278} :answer-field/id #uuid "eebbd260-66b0-55ac-b9f9-24d9e4e96e0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161276}] :question/id #uuid "fd780784-8f83-5b82-bd42-f51c45f9bf15" :question/math-academy-id "q-95727" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{11} & {v}_{12} \\\\ {v}_{21} & {v}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|?$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=3$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2.$ So, the second column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{2}=2.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=4.$ \n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-4I=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Seeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-4I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|=|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}|+|\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}|=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369790 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d64e4ef8-57ed-5743-bca7-960e44b7b29b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369792} {:answer/id #uuid "1af36dbf-7070-5476-b26d-797d1e243e7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369793} {:answer/id #uuid "aa2d111d-9382-5bea-bbb0-592e17ea1c1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369794} {:answer/id #uuid "15b38ce4-1327-54ae-a86f-78a638aa2402" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369795} {:answer/id #uuid "e0b1c6e0-7b4f-536e-95e8-2935f82068be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369793} :answer-field/id #uuid "e39c1d66-6a3d-5e7b-8424-ecf7645db688" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369791}] :question/id #uuid "a8011ac4-608e-5e35-aa57-26efa2ffe853" :question/math-academy-id "q-140636" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}13 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 12\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{11} & {v}_{12} \\\\ {v}_{21} & {v}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|?$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=13$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=12.$ So, the first column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{1}=13.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=169.$ \n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}153 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 160\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-169I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-169I=\\begin{bmatrix}153 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 160\\end{bmatrix}-169\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & -12 \\\\ -12 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Seeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-169I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|=|-\\frac{3}{5}|+|\\frac{4}{5}|=\\frac{7}{5}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369797 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "718aa4ab-c6a2-553c-b8b7-f653cc2600bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369799} {:answer/id #uuid "7ccf99e8-17c0-5a99-b7ab-53abb5528a2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369800} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d5b0b2-a831-5448-863b-c87e395627ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{