olution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-100I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|=|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371332 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b660dca2-4ca0-5220-9afe-a9076fa7f8e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371334} {:answer/id #uuid "ce67d9df-4bd2-5a78-8e66-b4c709d6c82d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371335} {:answer/id #uuid "dd9aa46a-026e-57a2-82a4-ce39c0ebc8be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371336} {:answer/id #uuid "9c54d3bd-417c-54c8-9f91-a3f0633cad1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371337} {:answer/id #uuid "da61597e-fd43-5b36-b772-f50c15912527" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{13}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371335} :answer-field/id #uuid "a23b8009-d363-5ace-91d8-9b9ed023f43a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371333}] :question/id #uuid "01aea047-32df-527d-acda-292ec1b804c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-96248" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{11} & {v}_{12} \\\\ {v}_{21} & {v}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|?$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=7$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=3.$ So, the first column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{1}=7.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=49.$ \n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}41 & -16 \\\\ -16 & 17\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-49I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-49I=\\begin{bmatrix}41 & -16 \\\\ -16 & 17\\end{bmatrix}-49\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -16 \\\\ -16 & -32\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Seeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-49I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|=|-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}|=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385872 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e37dfd1-4a21-5be4-90b0-f4412f73f3b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385874} {:answer/id #uuid "470ca91e-ccd2-5bab-b6e6-0b891e317ed6" :answer/type {:db/ident