y organized so that the variables are in the order $u,v$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient.   \n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables: \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524334 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ea54f46-cd9d-5f1a-8e7d-0bb9d5e691b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 7 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -12 & 12\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524341} {:answer/id #uuid "9762c4ad-74fe-5f46-ab05-155041c4f0c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -12 & 12\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524346} {:answer/id #uuid "b28fc242-5911-5dee-851c-180ffc4d677d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -12\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524349} {:answer/id #uuid "c89de38b-bfde-5ef4-aeb6-68f3fecf7bd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -12\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524352} {:answer/id #uuid "de60478b-74f8-522b-91a1-135db37da9ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -12\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -12\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524352} :answer-field/id #uuid "819139e3-9730-5d16-ae93-5334345832d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524343}] :question/id #uuid "46d3999d-feca-57b7-832c-c953e5a39c71" :question/problem "What is the coefficient matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{2}+7{x}_{1} &=2{x}_{3} \\\\ -12{x}_{3} &=12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}7{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}-12{x}_{3} &=12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524339 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "452a2c44-7316-5a22-b38c-9472d5277b1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 0 & 14 \\\\ -7 & -5 & 0 & -7\\end{array}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524333} {:answer/id #uuid "52fb4ff5-e6f6-500c-b68b-1f0e9bc219ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -14 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 7 & -7\\end{array}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524336} {:answer/id #uuid "71628b15-6d40-5ac1-874b-5bb32e0fe7cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & 14 \\\\ 7 & -5 & -7\\end{array}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524337} {:answer/id #uuid "c58bc993-c1a0-522b-a770-d35e63a11ebc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -1 & 14 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 7 & -7\\end{array}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524351} {:answer/id #uuid "dbbec889-e859-5f63-89a1-795e2ff0b381" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -14 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 7 & 7\\end{array}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -14 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 7 & -7\\end{array}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524336} :answer-field/id #uuid "77477f59-6d6c-5c9d-bb90-019a37bbb1aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524340}] :question/id #uuid "747811c9-1d46-520c-89bf-5eb46cd226f1" :question/problem "What is the augmented matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}a-c &=14b \\\\ 7c-5a &=-7\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered $a,b,c$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1a-14b-1c &=0 \\\\ -5a+0b+7c &=-7\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -14 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -14 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 7 & -7\\end{array}\\right]\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524344 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "416c8e1f-7111-581e-a509-7b34b0a57673" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 7 & -12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524330} {:answer/id #uuid "417160ba-08ca-5188-93fa-29957277b5f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 15 & 12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524331} {:answer/id #uuid "81566d40-fb1d-5a27-8685-3420bcae8beb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 7 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524342} {:answer/id #uuid "957414b9-92f6-54f1-8aca-322b35f2cbd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 7 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524345} {:answer/id #uuid "9e8bf4e3-2efc-51b4-8c05-6e39a53fce06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 15 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 7 & -12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524330} :answer-field/id #uuid "14c9b88d-aec0-5499-b8cd-b8bbd5e43493" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524326}] :question/id #uuid "91ed7eba-bb6a-5248-afd6-95d455e7e0d1" :question/problem "What is the coefficient matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}7{x}_{2}+15{x}_{1} &=12{x}_{3} \\\\ -6{x}_{3} &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}15{x}_{1}+7{x}_{2}-12{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}-6{x}_{3} &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 7 & -12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524357 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0770223c-8b5c-5636-b3e9-5880e25e3429" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 14 & -13 & -1\\end{array}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524325} {:answer/id #uuid "21607619-dc6d-59d0-9e81-d0f56feac876" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ -14 & -13 & 0 & -1\\end{array}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524327} {:answer/id #uuid "4957f688-bf0d-515e-885d-c76f80183421" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -6 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -13 & 0 & 14 & -1\\end{array}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524335} {:answer/id #uuid "7276986e-1f3d-5bf1-8018-452686a93d4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -6 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -13 & 0 & 14 & 1\\end{array}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524338} {:answer/id #uuid "d0d70aba-bca7-537e-aaf9-721b326dc164" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -1 & 6 \\\\ -13 & 0 & 14 & -1\\end{array}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -6 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -13 & 0 & 14 & -1\\end{array}\\right]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524335} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbc9b198-9031-5557-a635-483f8ef88236" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524353}] :question/id #uuid "dcfb9046-7853-50cf-9df9-12ba9e3679bb" :question/problem "What is the augmented matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}a-c &=6b \\\\ 14c-13a &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered $a,b,c$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1a-6b-1c &=0 \\\\ -13a+0b+14c &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 & -1 \\\\ -13 & 0 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -6 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -13 & 0 & 14 & -1\\end{array}\\right]\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524359 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27c09833-2403-5ffc-b739-1f7ef3f70509" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 11 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524328} {:answer/id #uuid "296aca9c-498e-5619-af5b-1bcfdeac6c9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 11 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -10 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524329} {:answer/id #uuid "44da178b-b353-59cb-86ac-5acfa946a32a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 11 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524332} {:answer/id #uuid "9a11e128-77bd-5f78-99f6-8a944b6bc1e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 15 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -10 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524347} {:answer/id #uuid "b36a9d4a-3d2c-55f4-bedc-7179b69a07c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 15 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 11 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524328} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdee999d-919a-557e-99e3-81b83833a2fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524354}] :question/id #uuid "f13c080e-707a-527d-993c-2b6cabc482fa" :question/problem "What is the coefficient matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}11{x}_{2}+15{x}_{1} &=6{x}_{3} \\\\ -10{x}_{3} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}15{x}_{1}+11{x}_{2}-6{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}-10{x}_{3} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 11 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Augmented Matrix of a Linear System With Variables Other Than x, y, z"}] :topic/math-academy-id 148 :topic/title "Systems of Equations as Augmented Matrices"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167634} :activity/id #uuid "5d719a4c-ab32-5d8b-b2a7-90e293dc6083" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167634 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168070 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $A$ is a $m\\times n$ matrix, and \n\n$$\nA=U \\Sigma V^T\n$$\n\nis a singular value decomposition of $A.$ If the $i$th singular value of $A$ is nonzero, then the $i$th column of the orthogonal matrix $U$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_i = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma_i} A\\mathbf{v}_i,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $\\mathbf u_i$ and $\\mathbf v_i$ are the $i$th columns of the orthogonal matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively.  \n\nThe formula above works whenever $\\sigma_i\\neq 0.$ But how do we find the $i$th column of $U$ in cases where $\\sigma_i = 0?$ It turns out that we can find the corresponding left singular vector using the geometric properties of singular values.\n\nFor example, suppose that\n\n![](images/fa/fab8a223fc22100592865eeb8b147313f4a635ffa3791863826ca6330730cc58.svg)\n\nand $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ where\n![](images/58/58af793e95fd96171530b2b63e74d12e965fb02eb81af42c8fdb3c9289a04b11.svg)\nThe matrices $\\Sigma$ and $V$ can be found using the methods we've seen before. So, our task now is to find the matrix $U.$ \n\nFrom $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that the singular values of $A$ are $\\sigma_1=4$ and $\\sigma_2=0.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 3 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the second singular value is $\\sigma_2=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ However, we know that $U$ is an orthogonal matrix. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n![](images/78/781532700996b7a60101f204b934483da264ecab6acd6bfeda97141669308b6c.svg)\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u}_2 = 0 \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\|\\mathbf{u}_2\\|=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's consider each condition in turn:\n\n- The equation $\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u}_2 = 0$  is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{{2}} x_1 +  \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} x_2 = 0.\n$$\n\nSeeking a nonzero solution to this equation, we get the vector \n![](images/75/7521799052487c5668d209c97ad6779f4258ecc5710ac81f2135966bf39974d3.svg)\n\n- Normalizing the vector we just found, we get\n![](images/30/30cc0b825690ed4dcd0bd672563f37bc6e621be092db70c4b46dafbf22db10c4.svg)\n\nTherefore, our orthogonal matrix $U$ is given by\n\n![](images/92/92ca53d48aed0c8435f89db4e11c022bc27ecb7a13d741e03270e0c981f639ef.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "01e70dc7-d85e-5e8c-939b-e7cd2c8b9ec3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5024 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5235213b-71c0-5372-9b7e-3f7b5abf5c6b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167636}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167635 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30b55397-ef25-5e49-bee0-eb619e97b6ad"} :step/id #uuid "b8cbe873-c1e3-5e8a-a689-8e8116d5b70e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167636 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99270a04-7542-5545-92a3-cf13464ef8bc"} :step/id #uuid "86c7507c-0f40-558d-b0f8-9cb4d481e270" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167637}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f0dc37d-a22e-572c-933d-f3490ad356de"} :step/id #uuid "af4cc601-95a5-50bc-8b95-471cf44bc75f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167657}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168073 :tutorial/content "It follows from the definitions that a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ has two repeated singular values $\\sigma_1= \\sigma_2= \\sigma$ if and only if the matrix  $A^TA$ has two repeated eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2 = \\sigma^2.$  Thus, since $A^TA$ is diagonalizable (because it is symmetric), the eigenspace corresponding to $\\sigma^2$ must be two-dimensional. Therefore, \n\n![](images/df/df266614e2576526353248439b7a17431d5e9e43153e1ba225adab3a89321373.svg)\n\nConsequently, we can use any pair of unit orthogonal vectors as a basis for the eigenspace corresponding to $\\sigma^2.$ \n\nLet's illustrate this with an example. Consider the following matrix:\n\n![](images/ee/ee74c3d4ed7e32388d06b47ed79789d768b54dee61e64d6db57c3eeb582089ae.svg)\n\nLet's find a singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}13 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: Notice that since $A^T \\! A$ is diagonal, the singular values of $A$ are \n\n$$\n\\sigma_1 = \\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{13}.\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$): Since the singular values are equal, we can always take as solutions of $(A^T\\!A-13I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ the unit eigenvectors \n![](images/d1/d1770843817b2557b032743e186dd27106374fef90a2a8e8bb3cfe77b2eb0398.svg)\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\mathbf u_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute $\\mathbf u_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U,$ $\\Sigma,$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/c9/c950cf3aff8f9ab47ede762fa9c4451c5e7fc1e78e12fca064ee87d89fcb571e.svg)\n\n**Note:** By swapping the order of eigenvectors in step 3, we swap the vectors $\\mathbf{u}_1$ and $\\mathbf{u}_2$ in step 4. However, the resulting matrices (with swapped columns) will give us a valid singular decomposition too." :tutorial/id #uuid "9a6bbc33-1584-5b2f-aab5-628e9c95743a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5040 :tutorial/title "Singular Value Decomposition of 2x2 Matrices With Repeated Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "967ac37b-6901-5b91-988c-19fc53224cc6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167635}}] :activity/title "Singular Value Decomposition of 2x2 Matrices With Zero or Repeated Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167041} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434258} :activity/id #uuid "1fc3e9bb-349e-5fb5-a883-56163930d8c0" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434257 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371388 :question/id #uuid "c0e502e2-3a05-5080-ba55-d3ba7ba5